# Define a public-safe reconstruction algorithm gate with bounded similarity

Treat the maintainer's complete lawful installation as the internal 100%
reference for coverage analysis, but do not generate the distributable algorithm
directly from that complete installation and do not publish an artifact that
embeds or reconstructs it without the user's lawful local source. Treat roughly
60% as the current planning estimate for a possible minimum installation and
45–55% as the tentative candidate-similarity window; validate both concepts
empirically before code uses them.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Specify what “100% installation”, “minimum installation”, and “similarity”
  mean in measurable, content-safe terms before choosing a production metric;
  treat the current ~60% minimum-installation estimate as planning only.
- [ ] Do not author a distributable `algorithm/*.txt` plan by serializing,
  full-tree diffing, or otherwise turning the complete 100% reference
  installation into direct recipe material.
- [ ] Keep the original user installation read-only throughout validation and
  reconstruction; algorithm execution writes only to SHAR-owned output/cache.
- [ ] Evaluate the tentative 45–55% similarity window; do not hardcode it until
  false-positive and false-negative evidence exists across lawful installs.
- [ ] Ensure passing similarity can never replace required local source bytes,
  exact-file gates, hashes, structural validation, or cryptographic/provenance
  checks.
- [ ] Ensure distributable family plans under `algorithm/*.txt` use the
  implemented `shar.algorithm.v1` source-bound contract and never become a
  reversible encoding of the maintainer's private complete installation.
- [ ] Keep plan-contained hashes limited to exact source binding and protected
  target verification. They are not an antivirus verdict and must never replace
  caller-supplied source bytes.
- [ ] Document the finalized metric and limitations in README when implemented;
  do not describe the tentative percentage as a legal safe harbor.

## Dependencies

Depends on representative source-installation evidence and final product
admission integration. The generic `shar.algorithm.v1` serialization mechanism
already exists and is not itself a similarity threshold.

## Evidence

`docs/technical/security/public-safe-reconstruction-gate.md` now defines the
100% reference, minimum installation, reference coverage, and a candidate
weighted-Jaccard similarity metric in content-safe count-vector terms. The
private reference contributes aggregate obfuscated directory/extension counts,
not payload bytes, file names, or a reversible full-tree recipe.

`tools/source-similarity/adapter-inbound/main.py` implements only evidence
measurement using exact rational arithmetic. Its regression suite covers
complete, missing, extra, invalid, collision-disambiguated, and non-generated
suffix vectors and proves the evidence object has no admission result. Generated
manifest collision ordinals are aggregated into their structural family before
comparison because a partial candidate can legitimately change those ordinals.
Hard manifest and deep validation remain exact and unchanged. No 45–55%
threshold exists in that code. The technical contract also pins the read-only
source boundary, source-bound plan publication restrictions, and the rule that
similarity cannot substitute for any hard source gate.

Repository publication policy now parses the substantive tracked icon plan and
every non-empty public family `algorithm/*.txt` plan, requiring the exact
`shar.algorithm.v1` top-level source/target structure, lowercase SHA-256 source
bindings, 96-bit nonce encoding, and non-empty hexadecimal protected payloads.
The current icon plan satisfies that guard with one source descriptor and seven
protected targets. The three platform game-family files remain deliberate empty
placeholders and are therefore not claimed as validated release plans. This
structural publication guard narrows what can be committed publicly; it does not
replace source-backed cryptographic replay evidence or the pending similarity
calibration.

The generic `shar.algorithm.v1` round-trip suite now includes explicit evidence
that a serialized plan cannot replay with zero caller-supplied source roots and
that successful create/replay leaves the caller source bytes and source layout
unchanged. Existing overlap guards continue to reject algorithm output inside a
source root. Authoring additionally rejects exact or nested source/target root
overlap in either containment direction before plan publication, preventing a
caller from turning source payloads into protected target material merely by
pointing the target back at the source tree. Authoring also rejects target files
that are hard-link aliases of any source file by cross-platform physical file
identity, closing a path-disjoint alias of the same payload. Source collection
also rejects repeated physical files before source file/byte limits are checked,
so hard-link aliases cannot inflate generic admission evidence. Target
collection rejects repeated physical files as unsupported hard-link topology
rather than replaying them as independent copies. Replay document validation
also rejects ancestor/descendant target path collisions before
decryption or writes, requiring one realizable protected output tree. Target
paths are compared by the repository's Unicode-uppercase portable identity, so
case-equivalent paths also fail before replay. Algorithm creation uses an atomic
create-new filesystem write, and a regression proves an existing plan remains
byte-identical when publication collides. Replay final files also use create-new
byte persistence, so an output appearing after the absence check cannot be
silently replaced. Generic filesystem failures are
projected as operation plus I/O error kind without caller paths, and a synthetic
missing-source regression proves a private-looking source path is not disclosed.
Authoring captures each admitted source/target file once, and key derivation
plus
target encryption reuse those validated bytes rather than reopening paths during
the operation. These tests establish engine-level source binding and read-only
behavior; they do not certify
the still-placeholder distributable family plans or replace the pending
representative-install similarity calibration.

The ~60% minimum-installation estimate and 45–55% similarity window remain
design targets, not validated production evidence, legal conclusions, or safe
harbors. The task remains open for representative-install calibration and final
algorithm serialization/admission integration.
